---
description: Copy .env.local into every new git worktree after creation
alwaysApply: true
---

# Worktree local env

Whenever you create a new git worktree (Cursor `~/.cursor/worktrees/…`,
`~/worktrees/<repo>/…`, `.worktrees/…`, `git worktree add`, `/start`, or any
native worktree tool), **immediately copy `.env.local` from the primary checkout
into the new worktree**.

```bash
# Primary checkout = first entry from `git worktree list`
PRIMARY="$(git worktree list --porcelain | awk '/^worktree /{print $2; exit}')"
WT="<new-worktree-path>"

if [ -f "$PRIMARY/.env.local" ]; then
  cp "$PRIMARY/.env.local" "$WT/.env.local"
elif [ -f "$PRIMARY/.env" ] && [ ! -f "$WT/.env" ]; then
  # Fallback only when the repo uses `.env` instead of `.env.local`
  cp "$PRIMARY/.env" "$WT/.env"
fi
```

Rules:

- Prefer **copy**, not symlink — each worktree keeps its own file.
- Do **not** overwrite an existing `$WT/.env.local` unless the user asks.
- If the primary has no `.env.local` (and no `.env`), say so and continue — do not
  invent secrets.
- Never commit `.env.local` / `.env`.
- Apply this on Cursor-managed worktrees under `~/.cursor/worktrees/` too; those
  do not inherit ignored env files from the main checkout.
